finding: P1 src/extras/multiplayer/intent.ts:138-142,170-175,199-201; src/extras/multiplayer/intent-window.test.ts:114-166: the new ID window bounds only the receiver and moves the same session-length leak to both client stores and outgoing retries. Public IntentSender: record ID 1 at tick 4294967294, then one on-time operation per tick, route all Accepted messages, confirm/acknowledge every replication frame, and prune before receive as the example does. ID 1 pins watermark zero, IDs 2..66 are accepted, and every subsequent ID stays unaccepted outside the ID window. Independently reproduced with actual ReplicationSender and ClientPrediction: at confirmed tick 1000 receiver=65, sender=936, prediction=936; at confirmed tick 3000 receiver=65, sender=2936, prediction=2936. Every sender.flush resends the entire growing pending log. Thus the original sequence is not bounded across all three stores; it now also prevents later on-time operations from being accepted for essentially the whole session. The new permanent-gap test manually encodes packets rather than exercising IntentSender/ClientPrediction, so it never observes these two growing stores or retry traffic. All 24 focused intent/prediction/pacing tests pass despite this failure. A bounded receive window needs a corresponding bounded client admission/backpressure or explicit failure contract, rather than silently accumulating arbitrarily many retryable records.
